  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:JzccAFXcTqE",
    "purpose": "Introduce Kenji's new super-nitrided carbon steel wok, designed with Our Place, and demonstrate it by cooking a simple Spam and egg fried rice, with discussion of wok materials, wok flavor and cleaning.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Kenji presents the carbon steel wok he spent a year developing with Our Place. Its super nitride treatment is meant to keep carbon steel's handling while reducing the usual care burden. A deliberately simple Spam and egg fried rice lets the wok's high-heat flavor be the main seasoning.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "The new wok is carbon steel with a 'super nitride' treatment, described as about twice as thick as a normal nitride layer, giving a slick, scratch-resistant, non-toxic surface.",
      "Kenji explains why he prefers carbon steel for woks: thin and responsive, heat stays concentrated at the bottom, lighter and faster to heat than cast iron.",
      "The nitride treatment addresses carbon steel's main downside, its seasoning upkeep and rust risk.",
      "Oil the wok first and use the smoke as the indicator that it's hot enough; ideally rub the oil in while the wok is cold.",
      "Use day-old rice spread on a sheet tray and chilled overnight so it dries and breaks apart.",
      "Render and crisp the diced Spam, then add scallions, rice and eggs.",
      "Season with white pepper, a salt and MSG blend, and a little soy sauce drizzled around the hot sides so it scorches rather than soaks in.",
      "Coated nonstick pans can't produce wok flavor, both because they don't get hot enough and because the food doesn't contact the metal.",
      "Clean it like any carbon steel pan: rinse, wipe, and heat it dry on the burner."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Introducing the new wok",
        "summary": "Kenji introduces the carbon steel wok he developed over a year with Our Place and explains its super nitride treatment.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Preparing the spam",
        "summary": "He dices Spam and talks about cooking on a river rafting trip, Spam's reputation and price. He explains he is keeping the ingredients simple so the wok flavor comes through.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Wok cooking advantages",
        "summary": "Using a portable butane burner (induction also works), he explains why carbon steel beats aluminum, clad and cast iron for woks. He notes how the nitride treatment removes the usual seasoning and rust worries.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "Cooking the fried rice",
        "summary": "He heats oil until smoking, scrambles eggs, renders the Spam, and explains why day-old rice works best. He recounts his old Target wok, then adds scallions, rice and eggs and explains why nonstick can't give wok flavor.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 9
      },
      {
        "title": "Seasoning and tasting",
        "summary": "He covers the high heat the wok can reach and seasons with white pepper, a salt and MSG blend, and soy sauce drizzled on the hot sides to scorch. Then he tastes and slides the rice out.",
        "first_segment": 10,
        "last_segment": 11
      },
      {
        "title": "Cleaning and wrap-up",
        "summary": "He shows cleaning: rinse, wipe and heat dry. He recaps the dish's ingredients and says how excited he is about the wok.",
        "first_segment": 11,
        "last_segment": 12
      }
    ],
